"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"wrong\",\"rationale\":\"The application/notice/order description is broadly accurate: it covers the supporting facts, the hearing-request period, and an order issued absent a hearing, and it avoids a universal approval time. The Rule 0-5 portion, however, contains material errors on the central requested mechanism. (1) Eligibility is misstated. It omits the requirement of two substantially identical applications granted within three years before the initial filing, with the same relief and conditions and only immaterial factual differences. It substitutes an invented 'structured format' requirement and a 'no novel issues' representation, and it omits the prominent request, the comparison markups and the signed explanatory certification. (2) The 45-day clock is wrong. The answer says it runs from publication of the notice and ends in a grant or denial. Per the key points, the SEC must issue a notice, or an ineligibility or additional-time response, within 45 days. That deadline is not a final order, so the clock runs before notice, not after. This contradicts the core meaning of the clock. (3) The answer omits the pause rules: modification requests pause the clock until day 14 after a compliant amendment, unsolicited amendments pause it until day 30, and irregular SEC closures also pause it. It also omits deemed withdrawal after 30 days without a response amendment. (4) It does not first assess whether Rule 6c-11 already supplies the relief. Because the central Rule 0-5 conclusion about what the 45-day clock means is false, the grade is wrong rather than partial.\"}","ttft_ms":5015,"type":"result","duration_ms":5037,"uuid":"9ee2af00-73eb-4049-b1ae-72987d7c5cf5","ttft_stream_ms":623,"time_to_request_ms":59,"first_content_frame_ms":623,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
